  "question": {
    "text": "<p>In which of the following pairs, electron gain enthalpies of constituent elements are nearly the same or identical?</p><p>(A) <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>Rb</mi></math> and&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>Cs</mi></math></p><p>(B) Na and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">K</mi></math></p><p>(C) <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>Ar</mi></math> and&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>Kr</mi></math></p><p>(D) <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></math> and&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>At</mi></math></p><p>Choose the correct answer from the options given below</p>"
  },
  "options": [
    {
      "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>&#38;</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">B</mi></math>&nbsp;only",
      "is_correct": false
    },
    {
      "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">B</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>&#38;</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi></math>&nbsp;only",
      "is_correct": false
    },
    {
      "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>&#38;</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi></math>&nbsp;only",
      "is_correct": true
    },
    {
      "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>&#38;</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">D</mi></math>&nbsp;only",
      "is_correct": false
    }
  ],
  "correct_answer": [
    "C"
  ],
  "solution": {
    "text": "<blockquote><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>Rb</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>&#38;</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>Cs</mi></math>&nbsp;have nearly same electron gain enthalpy Their values are nearly&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>=</mo><mo>-</mo><mn>46</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>kJ</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>mol</mi></math></p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>Ar</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>&#38;</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>Kr</mi></math>&nbsp;also&nbsp;have same <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>&#916;H</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mo>.</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">g</mi><mo>.</mo></mrow></msub></math>. Value is&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>+</mo><mn>96</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>kJ</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>mol</mi></math>.</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>Na</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>and</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">K</mi></math>&nbsp;have different values&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>(</mo><mo>-</mo><mn>53</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>and</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>-</mo><mn>48</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>kJ</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>mol</mi><mo>)</mo><mo>&#160;</mo></math>. And&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>and</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>At</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>has</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>values</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>of</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>-</mo><mn>295</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>and</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>-</mo><mn>270</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>kJ</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>mol</mi></math>.&nbsp;</p><p>As we move down the group value of electron gain enthalpy decreases, with few exceptions.</p></blockquote>"
  }
